                <h2>Case Opening: A Utility App's Cold Start Dilemma</h2>
<p>A utility app team launched on TikTok Shop US market in early March with a $300 weekly budget, but only got 12 conversions with an ROI as low as 0.3. Operations manager Xiaolin found: high material repetition, user dwell time under 3 seconds, and comment section full of "useless" and "scam" remarks.</p>
<p>This wasn't a quality issue—it was a material pool that was never properly built.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Breakdown: Three Predicaments of Cold Start Failure</h2>
<h3>Predicament 1: Severely Insufficient Materials</h3>
<p>Many teams launch with only 5-8 materials, believing "quality over quantity." But TikTok's algorithm needs sufficient materials for A/B testing and traffic distribution. Too few materials means the system can't find optimal audience matching, causing overall delivery difficulties.</p>
<p><strong>Judgment Method</strong>: Open Ads Manager and check the exposure distribution of materials under each ad group. If 80% of traffic concentrates on 1-2 materials, the material pool depth is insufficient.</p>
<h3>Predicament 2: Monotonous Material Structure</h3>
<p>Many beginners fall into a trap: since certain video types perform well, they copy them all. Such homogenized materials cause aesthetic fatigue and fail to cover users in different interest circles.</p>
<p><strong>Action</strong>: Classify materials into three types:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Product Showcase</strong>: Display product usage scenarios and effect comparisons</li>
<li><strong>Review Type</strong>: Emphasize authentic experience and credibility</li>
<li><strong>UGC Style</strong>: Simulate the tone of spontaneous user recommendations</li>
</ul>
<p>Maintain a 4:3:3 ratio for these three types, ensuring coverage for users at different decision stages.</p>
<h3>Predicament 3: No Material Lifecycle Management</h3>
<p>A common mistake: stopping a material after 2 days of poor data without giving the system enough learning time; or not iterating a material that has run 50,000 exposures.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended SOP</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li>First 48 hours: observe only, no intervention, let the system complete its learning phase</li>
<li>Materials with 10,000+ exposures but no conversions: mark for optimization or pause</li>
<li>High-performing materials: create secondary edits (different opening, music, subtitles) to extend lifecycle</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Actions Taken: Rebuilding the Material Pool in Two Weeks</h2>
<p>The team did three things:</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Expand Material Types</strong><br />
Expanded from 8 to 30 materials, including 15 re-edited original videos, 8 factory-provided scenario videos, and 7 user feedback materials.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Establish Material Tagging System</strong><br />
Tag each material with "Audience Tag - Product Selling Point - Content Format" in three dimensions for quick filtering and combination delivery later.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Set Material Iteration Rhythm</strong><br />
Check material data at a fixed time daily (recommended 3 PM), eliminate materials with CTR below 1%, and add 5-8 new materials to the test group weekly.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Results Comparison: Key Changes from ROI 0.3 to 1.8</h2>
<p>Two-week review data:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Cold Start Week 1</th>
<th>After Adjustment Week 2</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Material Count</td>
<td>8 pieces</td>
<td>30+ pieces</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Average CTR</td>
<td>0.8%</td>
<td>2.1%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>$28</td>
<td>$9</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ROI</td>
<td>0.3</td>
<td>1.8</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The key turning point was week three—when material pool depth exceeded 20 pieces, the system began automatically distributing traffic to better-performing material combinations, bringing the overall account into a positive cycle.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Practical Advice: Three Reminders for Operators</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Don't heavily invest budget before the material pool is ready</strong>. The system needs sufficient test materials to understand the product model. Warm-up period budget should be controlled at 30%-50% of target budget.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Material iteration is more important than creation</strong>. Instead of spending time shooting new videos, try changing the opening of existing materials, adjusting subtitle sequences, or replacing background music—this often directly improves CTR by 20%-30%.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Focus on video completion rate, not just click rate</strong>. TikTok's algorithm weighs completion rate heavily. For 15-30 second videos, if the first 3 seconds don't capture users, great content afterward is wasted.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>The essence of cold start is "using sufficient materials to exchange for the algorithm's understanding of the product." The material pool doesn't need to be bigger, but it must be deep enough, diverse enough, and well-iterated.</p>
